---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

/*
  English-language description of the product.

  Exists for readers who do not read Norwegian. In practice that means
  Google's OAuth verification reviewers, who rejected the Norwegian home page
  with "does not explain the purpose of your app". Everything user-facing stays
  Norwegian; this page is the one exception, and is linked from the footer.

  Keep it accurate rather than promotional: it is read by people deciding
  whether to trust the application with an identity provider.
*/
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Myntos: free Norwegian bookkeeping software"
  description="Free Norwegian bookkeeping software that will stay free: receipts, invoices with EHF, the VAT return, a limited company's tax return and the SAF-T export."
  lang="en"
>
  {/* A wrapper, not a second <main>: the layout's is the page's landmark, and
      the document's language comes from lang above. */}
  <div>
    <h1>About Myntos</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      Myntos is a free, web-based <strong>bookkeeping application for Norwegian companies</strong>.
      It records incoming and outgoing payments with their supporting documents, issues invoices,
      calculates value-added tax, and produces the reports Norwegian law requires: the VAT return,
      the tax return of a limited company, and the SAF-T Financial export for the Norwegian Tax
      Administration (Skatteetaten).
    </p>

    <h2>What you can do with it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Register a company</strong> by its Norwegian organisation number. The chart of
        accounts, VAT codes and financial years are set up automatically.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Record payments in and out</strong>, each with a type that determines its account
        and VAT treatment, and a PDF or photo of the receipt or invoice, which Myntos reads into the
        form for you to confirm.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Issue invoices</strong> checked against the Norwegian bookkeeping regulations, each
        with its EHF e-invoice file.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Prepare the VAT return</strong> for each period, checked by Skatteetaten before it
        is filed, and <strong>file a limited company's tax return</strong> (skattemelding with
        næringsspesifikasjon) through Altinn, signed in with ID-porten.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reconcile the bank</strong> by importing account statements (camt.053), and close
        the year.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Work as a team.</strong> Access is granted per company: colleagues and accountants
        are invited into one set of books with a role, and an accountant can keep every client on
        one account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Move in and out.</strong> Earlier years come in from other Norwegian programs as
        SAF-T files, and the whole ledger goes out the same way. SAF-T export and the document
        archive are legal requirements, not premium features.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Not built yet: payroll and the a-melding, the annual accounts to the Register of Company
      Accounts, the shareholder register return, recording a customer's payment against an
      invoice, and sending e-invoices over Peppol. The full list
      is at the end of <a href="/funksjoner/#ikke-enna" lang="nb">Funksjoner</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>
      Small Norwegian companies, sole proprietorships and limited companies (AS), and the
      accountants who keep their books. It is built around Norwegian rules first: the data model
      follows SAF-T Financial, corrections are made by reversing entries as
      bokføringsloven requires, and entries cannot be edited after posting.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing, and that will not change: Myntos is free for good. There is no price list, no paid
      tier and no trial period, and it takes no payment details. Filing to Altinn and Skatteetaten
      costs the same, and so will everything added later. We do not sell or share the books or use
      them to train models. The source code is public, so you can also run Myntos yourself. The
      full answer, in Norwegian, is on <a href="/priser/" lang="nb">Priser</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Signing in, and what we do with your Google account</h2>
    <p>
      You can create an account with an email address and password. You may also sign in with
      Google, in which case we receive only your <strong>name</strong> and
      <strong>email address</strong> from Google. Microsoft sign-in works the same way where it is
      offered.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where BankID sign-in is offered, it goes through <strong>Idura</strong>, which is part of
      Stø, the company behind BankID. From BankID we receive your name and a pseudonymous identifier, and
      nothing else that we keep: no email address, and we do not ask for your national identity
      number (fødselsnummer). A BankID is attached to an existing account only when you do it
      yourself while signed in, from your account page. An account that signs in with BankID
      signs in with BankID only, as BankID's terms require: attaching it removes the account's
      password and any Google or Microsoft sign-in.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those two values are used for one purpose: to create your Myntos account and to identify you
      when you sign in again. We do not read, send or store anything else from your account with
      that provider, we do not request access to your files, mail or contacts, and we do not sell
      or share the data with third parties. You can remove a Google or Microsoft connection at any
      time and keep signing in with a password, and switch an account from BankID to email and
      password from your account page. See <a href="/personvern/">our privacy policy</a> for the
      full account.
    </p>

    <h2>Where your data is kept</h2>
    <p>
      The database and the document archive both run inside the <strong>EEA</strong>: the database
      in Frankfurt, Germany, and the document storage in an EU-jurisdiction bucket.
      Bokføringsforskriften § 7-5 permits electronic accounting records to be kept anywhere in the
      EEA provided the bookkeeping-obligated entity notifies the tax office in writing, and Myntos
      is designed to help you send that notice rather than pretend the requirement does not exist.
    </p>

    <h2>Source code</h2>
    <p>
      The source code of Myntos is
      <a href="https://github.com/andreasviner/myntos" rel="noopener"><strong>publicly available</strong></a>
      so that anyone can inspect exactly how the numbers are produced, which matters more for accounting software than for
      most things. The code is source-available under the
      <a href="https://github.com/andreasviner/myntos/blob/main/LICENSE" rel="noopener">Elastic License 2.0</a>: you may read, run, modify and
      self-host it at no cost and without an agreement with us, but not offer it to others as a
      hosted service. That is not an open-source licence.
    </p>

    <h2>Who operates it</h2>
    <p>
      Myntos is operated by <strong>Lindeman Technologies</strong>, Norway. For questions about the
      service or this page, the contact details are on
      <a href="/personvern/">the privacy page</a>. The service is provided without warranty; see
      <a href="/ansvar/">our disclaimer</a> and <a href="/vilkar/">terms</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="card-hint">
      <a href="/">← Til den norske forsiden</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
